course: dictionary
lesson: 12079
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cloakroom is a room where you leave your coat, bag or umbrella, so you do not have to carry it around. Schools and theatres often have one. Read this: «Hang your wet coat in the cloakroom before you go into class.» The clues are coat and hang, so a cloakroom must be a place for your things.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Excuse me, where is the ladies' cloakroom?» could mean a room for coats, but in polite British talk it often means a toilet. The words around it are the context, so look at them. Is there a coat or a bag nearby, or is someone asking for the way?
explain: Polite words are sometimes used instead of plainer ones. Toilet is plain, but cloakroom is a softer, politer word. Always test your meaning in the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A cloakroom is a room where you can leave coats, bags and umbrellas.
fact: In a theatre, you often leave your coat at the cloakroom and get a ticket to collect it later.
fact: In polite British talk, «the ladies' cloakroom» can mean the toilet.
fact: A euphemism is a softer, polite word used instead of a plainer one.
fact: The meaning of a word often depends on the words around it, so check the context before you decide.
exercise: «Please hang your wet coat in the cloakroom.» Where should the coat go? | The sea | *A room for coats and bags | The garden | A shop window
exercise: «We left our bags in the cloakroom and got a ticket at the theatre.» What is a cloakroom in this sentence? | *A room to leave coats and bags | A stage for actors | A place to sell tickets | A cafe
exercise: «Excuse me, where is the ladies' cloakroom?» In polite British talk, what might this mean? | A cupboard for shoes | A swimming pool | A room for umbrellas only | *A toilet, said politely
test: «Put your umbrella and school bag in the cloakroom.» What can you leave in a cloakroom? | Books only | *Umbrellas and bags | Only shoes | Sandwiches only
test: Mum says, «I'll be in the cloakroom for a minute» at a restaurant. Which meaning is most likely? | *A toilet, said politely | A room for cooking | A bus stop | A room for coats only
exam: «The cloakroom was full, so Sam hung his coat on the hook outside.» What does cloakroom mean? | A park with benches | A kitchen for cooking | *A room to leave coats, bags and umbrellas | A bus stop
